**Solution Overview**

We walk through the list once, keeping track of the two largest distinct values we have seen so far:

| Variable | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `first`  | The highest number encountered up to the current position |
| `second` | The second‑highest distinct number encountered up to the current position |

For each element `x` we update these two variables according to the following rules:

1. If `x` is greater than `first` → `second = first; first = x`.
2. Else if `x` is **different** from `first` and greater than `second` → `second = x`.

At the end, `second` holds the second highest distinct value.  
If the input list contains fewer than two distinct numbers we throw an `InvalidOperationException` (you can change this to return a sentinel value if you prefer).

The algorithm runs in **O(n)** time and **O(1)** extra space, and it does not use any built‑in sorting helpers.

---

### C# Implementation

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public static class NumberUtils
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Returns the second highest distinct integer in the sequence.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="numbers">A list of integers.</param>
    /// <returns>The second highest distinct value.</returns>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentNullException">If <paramref name="numbers"/> is null.</exception>
    /// <exception cref="InvalidOperationException">
    /// If the list contains fewer than two distinct values.
    /// </exception>
    public static int SecondHighest(IList<int> numbers)
    {
        if (numbers == null)
            throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(numbers));

        // We need at least two distinct values.
        if (numbers.Count < 2)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("The list must contain at least two elements.");

        // Initialise with the smallest possible int so any real value will be larger.
        int first  = int.MinValue;   // highest value seen so far
        int second = int.MinValue;   // second‑highest distinct value seen so far

        foreach (int x in numbers)
        {
            if (x > first)
            {
                // New maximum found – shift the old max down to second place.
                second = first;
                first  = x;
            }
            else if (x < first && x > second) // x is distinct from first and better than current second
            {
                second = x;
            }
            // If x == first we ignore it because we want distinct values.
        }

        // After the loop, if second is still int.MinValue it means we never found a distinct
        // value smaller than the maximum (e.g., all elements are the same).
        if (second == int.MinValue)
            throw new InvalidOperationException("The list does not contain a second distinct value.");

        return second;
    }
}
```

#### How it works – step‑by‑step example

```csharp
var list = new List<int> { 7, 3, 9, 5, 9, 2 };
int result = NumberUtils.SecondHighest(list);
// result == 7
```

| Iteration | x | first | second | Explanation |
|-----------|---|-------|--------|-------------|
| start | – | `int.MinValue` | `int.MinValue` | – |
| 1 | 7 | 7 | `int.MinValue` | 7 > first → new max |
| 2 | 3 | 7 | 3 | 3 < first && 3 > second |
| 3 | 9 | 9 | 7 | 9 > first → shift 7 down |
| 4 | 5 | 9 | 7 | 5 < first but not > second |
| 5 | 9 | 9 | 7 | duplicate of first → ignored |
| 6 | 2 | 9 | 7 | unchanged |
| end | – | 9 | 7 | second highest = 7 |

---

### Edge‑case handling

| Situation | Behaviour |
|-----------|-----------|
| `null` list | Throws `ArgumentNullException` |
| List with < 2 elements | Throws `InvalidOperationException` |
| All elements equal (e.g., `{4,4,4}`) | Throws `InvalidOperationException` because there is no distinct second value |
| Negative numbers | Works fine (comparison uses normal integer ordering) |
| List containing `int.MinValue` | Still works because we start with `int.MinValue` and only replace it when a *greater* value appears. If `int.MinValue` is the only value, the method will correctly report “no second distinct value”. |

If you prefer a *sentinel* return value (e.g., `int.MinValue` or `null` via `int?`) instead of throwing, replace the exception at the end with:

```csharp
return second == int.MinValue ? (int?)null : second;
```

and change the method signature to `public static int? SecondHighest(IList<int> numbers)`.

